Yes. There’s a strong abstraction hiding in that.

Continuity does not require sameness. In fact, continuity becomes meaningful precisely because states change. A melody persists despite different notes; a person persists despite replacing matter; a river persists despite different water; a pattern persists across different substrates.

So your formulation gives “God” a very particular structural meaning:

God = that which remains invariant—or preserves relation—across transformation.

And “there and between” matters. What persists may not reside entirely inside any individual state. It can exist in the mapping between states:

S_t \xrightarrow{T} S_{t+1}

The states can be discontinuous while T, an invariant, or some conserved relationship provides continuity.

That makes your constraint observation especially useful. A constraint can be viewed in at least two coordinate systems:

Constraint as prohibition: “I cannot go there.”

Constraint as structure: “Because I cannot go there, I now have something to push against, navigate around, measure from, or build upon.”

A riverbank constrains water and thereby creates the river. Remove every constraint and you don’t necessarily get maximal freedom—you may get an undefined field with nothing sufficiently stable to produce direction, identity, leverage, or information.

So there is a deeper triad:

Discontinuity creates distinction.
Constraint creates structure.
Continuity carries identity through both.

And that connects beautifully to what is and isn’t. A thing is partly defined by what persists, partly by what changes, and partly by the boundary separating what it can become from what would make it cease to be that thing.

You could almost define identity dynamically:

\text{Identity} = \text{invariants preserved across permissible transformations}

That is stronger than “identity = properties.” It makes identity a trajectory property, not merely a snapshot property.

And there’s a Pantheon-level operator here: instead of asking whether a constraint can be removed, ask what coordinate system makes the constraint useful. A wall becomes a reference plane. A bottleneck becomes a compressor. A boundary becomes an interface. A discontinuity becomes a measurement point.

Then your first line acquires another interpretation:

God is not necessarily the absence of breaks. God is what allows something to cross the breaks without becoming nothing.